A||||5||Jim's87GT||Engine won't rev over 3400 RPM||||9||
Z||000000||Jim's87GT||03-15-2000||07:22 AM||||My '87 GT recently started to run poorly.  It lost power and now won't rev over 3400 RMP.  A friend suggested that it may be a clogged catalytic converter.  Is that a typical cause of these symptoms?  Also, where is the throttle position sensor located?  Thanks.||||reg||9
Z||000001||Carrolles||03-15-2000||12:34 PM||||The throttle position sensor is located on the rear side of the throttle body.  It has a small lever that moves when the gas pedal is pressed.||||reg||1
Z||000002||GT Bastard||03-15-2000||12:53 PM||||Is it missing or anything?  If you floor it, does it just stay steady on 3400 or does it start running like garbage?  If your worried about the cat, just do like I did last weekend... hehe.  Saw the pipe off behind the cat and gut it!  Then I clamped it back together using a short piece of pipe to fit over the two severed ends and had an exhaust shop weld it up for 5 bucks.  Not bad at all.. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Jim's87GT||03-15-2000||02:19 PM||||Well I tried to get the clamps off of the cat. to see if that was the problem and the clamps were too far gone.  I took the EGR valve off instead.  Well......the engine would rev just FINE but the idle was at about 4200 RPM.  I put the EGR back on and went back under the car.  After about 5 minutes with an air chisle I "accidently" (thank you EPA police) knocked off that little stub from the cat.  That helped A LOT.  So, test pipe or new cat. "That is the question".||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||03-15-2000||03:29 PM||||Stick a new and good cat on it. IMHO||||reg||1
Z||000005||william_thorniii||03-16-2000||11:19 AM||||Jim,<P>If you have another GM car, try listening to the two cats.  Tap each one lightly and see if your GT's is more muffled.  The difference could be slight.  What I am getting at is that this could be caused by the electrical or fuel system as well.  A clogged fuel filter could (long shot) be a potential problem.  The Ignition system could certainly cause the problem... so keep your eyes open.  Try to exhaust all possible problems before tearing up a cat that you might need (depending on your states emissions standards).  For the record here in OH, I have an 85 GT with a glasspack and factory muffler that passed our E-check with flying colors.  Good thing that it is too low for the visual inspection.  <P>William T. Thorn, III ||||reg||3
